{"id": "striviaqa0127", "pool": "striviaqa", "tier": "never", "audio_s": 8.43, "n_chunks": 9, "n_concurrent": 9, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.2911, "eot_read_ms": 29, "reference_answer": "apollo (accepted aliases: loxian apollo, apotropaeus, απόλλων, apollo delius, apollo coelispex, manticus apollo, lycoctonus, acraephius apollo, apollo phoebus, apóllon, apollo leschenorius, phoebos apollo, archigetes, didymaeu)", "query": "In Greek mythology, who did flute playing shepherd Marsyas challenge to a music contest, the winner of which flayed Marsyas to death for his presumption?", "transcript": "In Greek mythology, who did flute-playing shepherd Marsyas challenge to a music contest, the winner of which flayed Marsyas to death for his presumption?", "mode": "local", "answer": "In Greek mythology Marsyas the satyr challenged Apollo to a music contest. The winner of which Apollo flayed Marsyas to death for his presumption.", "answer_ms": 949}
{"id": "striviaqa0130", "pool": "striviaqa", "tier": "never", "audio_s": 19.51, "n_chunks": 20, "n_concurrent": 20, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.6672, "eot_read_ms": 29, "reference_answer": "library of congress (accepted aliases: u s library of congress, library of congress of united states, prints and photographs division, library of congress authorities, united states library of congress, congressional library, loc gov, wash)", "query": "From 1937 to 1942 Alan Lomax, who contributed more than 10,000 field recordings of folk music from the USA, the Caribbean, Ireland, Great Britain, Spain, and Italy, was Assistant in Charge of the Archive of Folk Song of what establishment?", "transcript": "From nineteen thirty seven to nineteen forty two Alan Lomax, who contributed more than ten thousand field recordings of folk music from the USA, the Caribbean, Ireland, Great Britain, Spain, and Italy, was assistant in charge of the Archive of Folk Song of what establishment?", "mode": "local", "answer": "From 1937 to 1942, Alan Lomax traveled across the USA, collecting more than ten thousand field recordings of folk music from the USA, the Caribbean, Ireland, Great Britain, Spain, and Italy. He was assistant in charge of the Archive of American Folksong at the Library of Congress. While there he met his future wife, classical pianist Eileen Tyler. Lomax was a tall man with an intense gaze. He liked wearing tweed jackets and smoking cigars. His work was meticulous. He took notes carefully, recorded every detail, and preserved songs that might otherwise have been lost. Lomax believed that folk music carried the stories and voices of ordinary people. He wanted to share those stories with the world. After his time in the US, Lomax continued traveling, collecting music from around the globe. He helped create a rich archive that scholars and musicians still use today. His passion for preserving cultural heritage inspired generations.", "answer_ms": 6788}
{"id": "striviaqa0133", "pool": "striviaqa", "tier": "never", "audio_s": 11.28, "n_chunks": 12, "n_concurrent": 12, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.6451, "eot_read_ms": 32, "reference_answer": "sucre (accepted aliases: chuquisaca sucre, sucre bolivia, sucre chuquisaca, sucre, historic city of sucre, constitutional capital of bolivia, sucre city, sucre oropeza, sucer)", "query": "French for an additive, and a Venezuelan liberator of Ecuador, Bolivia and Peru from Spain, what was the currency of Ecuador before adopting the US dollar in the early 2000s?", "transcript": "French fanatic, and a Venezuelan liberator of Ecuador, Bolivia, and Peru from Spain, what was the currency of Ecuador before adopting the US dollar in the early two thousands?", "mode": "local", "answer": "The dragon practiced folding and rolling the dough until it became smooth and elastic. It learned how to shape the dough into perfect circles for bread rolls, and then watched carefully as the baker placed them in the oven. The dragon even tried its own hand at kneading, but found that its tiny claws made quite a mess! Eventually, the baker let the dragon help by sprinkling flour on the counter and handing over small tools. By the end of the day, the dragon had helped make dozens of loaves, and its scales sparkled with flour. From that day on, the village bakery was known not just for its delicious bread, but for the little dragon who loved helping out every morning.", "answer_ms": 4990}
